---
const chain = [
	'Network intervention',
	'Customer experience',
	'Customer behavior',
	'Commercial outcome',
	'Incremental value',
];
---

<section aria-labelledby="focus-heading">
	<h2 id="focus-heading">What I'm thinking about now</h2>

	<p>
		I'm currently focused on value measurement: moving beyond operational improvements to quantify
		whether interventions causally influence customer behavior and commercial outcomes.
	</p>

	<div class="breakout">
		<div class="breakout-inner">
			<ol class="chain" aria-label="From network intervention to incremental value">
				{chain.map((step, i) => (
					<li>
						<span class="chain-step">{step}</span>
						{i < chain.length - 1 && (
							<span class="chain-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&darr;</span>
						)}
					</li>
				))}
			</ol>
		</div>
	</div>

	<p>
		Each arrow is a separate empirical claim. My <a href="/work/promo-causal">causal-inference case
		study</a> works through one such claim end to end on synthetic data with a known answer, and
		the same instinct to check a result against a known truth shows up in <a
			href="/work/agent-bench">how I evaluate AI-generated analysis</a>.
	</p>
</section>

<style>
	.breakout {
		width: 100vw;
		margin-left: calc(50% - 50vw);
		padding-inline: 1.375rem;
		margin-block: 1.75rem;
	}

	.breakout-inner {
		max-width: 960px;
		margin-inline: auto;
	}

	.chain {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: 0;
		max-width: 26rem;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: center;
	}

	.chain li {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: center;
		width: 100%;
	}

	.chain-step {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		text-align: center;
		padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--rule);
		border-radius: 6px;
		font-family: var(--serif);
		font-size: 1rem;
	}

	.chain li:last-child .chain-step {
		border-color: var(--accent);
		background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 10%, transparent);
	}

	.chain-arrow {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		line-height: 2;
	}
</style>
